hi there Steve Kaufman here again today  I want to talk about something that I  think is extremely important maybe the  most important thing when it comes to  the techniques of language learning I  talked a lot about the attitudes  required about the amount of time you  need to spend but today I want to talk  about techniques and the reason is  because there was a comment here on a  video that I did a long time ago where I  talked about vocabulary and I the title  of my video was can we learn a hundred  words a day which I believe you can  under certain circumstances and someone  said no because Lida Baker who is an ESL  expert thirty years of experience in  teaching English as a foreign language  says you should concentrate on two or  three no more so let me begin by saying  that I have always felt that the most  important task in language learning is  to acquire words words so that you can  understand what people are saying so  that you can eventually learn to use  these words the task of language  learning is accumulating words and you  need a lot of words I have always said  that the amount of words that you know  passively that you can understand that  is the single most important measurement  of your potential in a language because  the more words you know and understand  even passively the more you can read the  more you can listen the more meaningful  content you can actually expose yourself  to so when it comes to learning words  the idea that you should that leader  suggests that every word you encounter  you should write it on a piece of paper  and on the back again if you're an  English Learner  so therefore in your target language  write up a definition in the back in the  target language write up some synonyms  spend a lot of time on that word and do  two or three a day the problem with that  is that if you do two or three words a  day  that's not very  many words at the end of the year even  if you did 3 ohyea a day that's around a  thousand words problem is a that's not  very many and B you're not gonna  remember them because the learning  process is a process of learning and  forgetting learning and forgetting so  that's problem number one problem number  two is we learn words best in context  and so once you isolate this and create  your own little sentences and synonyms  you're divorced from any context what's  more not only do we learn where it's  better in context when we can associate  it with something we need multiple  contexts because we don't really  understand the meaning of a word and how  it's were used what it means until we've  seen it in many many different contexts  therefore we have to expose ourselves to  a lot of content alright and it's  obvious from my statistics at link that  I learn far more words incidentally  without deliberately trying to learn  them than I do  deliberately trying to learn them what  do I mean by that is that any language  that I have studied for any length of  time and linked my known words total the  number of words that I know which  usually means words that I have not  looked up that I passed by when I was  reading our link is far greater than the  number of words that I saved and then  went about learning because I  encountered them subsequently or  whatever and I moved their status to  known because as we advanced in a  language so we learn the word act and  maybe react and pretty soon we're kind  of getting a sense of this word so when  we see action or activity or activate  all of a sudden we don't have to look  those words up because we or we may look  them up but then very quickly you move  them to known because our power grows  because of that the sort of snowballing  effect of the more words you know  the more words you can guess the more  words them will naturally be added to  your known words total and even in  Chinese as you know more characters you  can then guess the meaning of new  compounds because most words in Chinese  consist of two two characters put  together so you're constantly acquiring  more and more words almost  subconsciously and if your strategy is  to focus on learning two or three words  a day many of which you're gonna forget  you won't learn very many words if I  look at my own experience for example  right now in Arabic so I should say too  that the number of words you can learn  per day is going to depend on how close  the language is to a language you  already know so if you're a speaker of a  Romance language you know if you learn  another Romance language you're gonna  learn words very very quickly  in two months I had a vocabulary in  Romanian of 20,000 words okay however  with Greek where there are far fewer  words that you know I recognize from  other languages I think I was learning  about 50 words a day and I was picking  up speed because again the more words I  know the more likely that I'm going to  encounter different forms of that word  and therefore I'm not gonna have to save  it and so my my vocabulary learning  starts to pick up speed but I was  running at about fifty words a day  now with Arabic I checked my statistics  I'm over 3,000 words three thousand two  hundred words or so I've been out of  about a hundred and fifty days so that's  about twenty a day but I know that I'm  gonna pick up speed in my Arabic so 20  words a day is is at a low point because  I'm struggling with an unfamiliar script  because I'm still trying to get a  toehold on the language because I don't  have many or if any or a very very few  identifiable words from other languages  but that's still 20 a day it's not two  or three a day because I know from  experience that to be comfortable in a  language I need 20,000 30,000 40,000  50,000 words granted it's the way we  count the middle  where we consider every form of the word  as a different word so it's not  something that I can go brag about that  I know so many words and I certainly  can't use all those words but it is a  bit of a measurement it's a milestone  it's it's also an incentive I want to  push that number up as as much as I can  because I know that if I start to reach  like right now I'm an hour Arabic I'm at  3,000 words I said I'm gonna start  speaking I have in fact had four  conversations with an Arabic tutor at  itok II and so 3,000 words had it to me  is normally much earlier than I'd like  to start speaking at I think in checked  our waited till I had five or six  thousand words it doesn't matter but I  know that to be comfortable in Arabic  I'm gonna need 20,000 words  if I read a newspaper article in Arabic  I'm looking up two-thirds of the words  so I'm nowhere near I have a sense for  the language I have some common  vocabulary but in order for me to be  where I want to be I can't be learning  at one or two or three words a day  adding into that the fact that I'm gonna  forget most of them this is the other  thing it's elusive you think you've  learned something you think you've  learned a word you know if you aren't  constantly reading and listening and  encountering that word in many different  situations you're gonna lose it and so  therefore the strategy has to be to not  worry too much about learning words I  don't spend a lot of time reviewing  words I review them after a lesson once  but I don't go through my long list of  saved words and then some kind of a you  know an effort to try and nail down  words in isolation from the the lesson I  review words immediately after a lesson  just to give me a bit of a chance or I  sometimes go back to a lesson that I've  read five six seven times and rather  than reading through the whole lesson I  just go through on link those words and  phrases that continue to give me trouble  but always connected to that context and  then I listen to the lesson so it's  always context can  so e-learning vocabulary to me is the  most important task you need lots of  words you need words in order to speak  to people otherwise you can't understand  what they're saying back to you and the  native speaker has a lot of words so you  have to at least understand those words  even if you use a smaller subset of  those words that you understand  passively so focus on learning words  learn the words through massive  listening and reading don't spend your  time as Lida Baker's suggests cutting  out making little cards and writing you  know how much time does that take like I  don't have if I had 810 hours a day I  might do what she suggested but I have  an hour or two a day the bulk of it is  simply listening I have to have a  strategy that enables me to learn words  naturally incidentally while doing those  tasks that I find enjoyable and that are  easy for me to do and I think her  strategy is not that and I will leave a  link to that article and I thank the  person who raised the comment or the  question to that YouTube video of mine  so thank you for listening bye for now  [Music]
 

